# Firebase

## 1. Overview

Firebase will be used as the backend service for the Learner Support Portal. It will provide user authentication and cloud-based data storage for the main features of the system.

The project will use:

* **Firebase Authentication** for learner registration and login.
* **Firebase Realtime Database** for storing application data.
* **Firebase REST API** for communication between the website and the database.
* **Firebase Security Rules** to control access to stored data.

The main information stored in Firebase will include learner profiles, tasks, support bookings, game scores and learning resources.

---

## 2. Firebase Authentication

Firebase Authentication will manage user accounts within the application.

Learners will be able to:

* Register a new account.
* Log in to their account.
* Log out of the application.
* Remain authenticated while using the portal.

Each Firebase Authentication account will have a unique `uid`. This ID will be used to connect the authenticated user with their records in the Realtime Database.

### Authentication Flow

```text
Learner
   ↓
Registration / Login
   ↓
Firebase Authentication
   ↓
Authenticated User
   ↓
User UID
   ↓
Learner Data in Realtime Database
```

Passwords will be managed by Firebase Authentication and will not be stored directly in the application's database.

---

## 3. Realtime Database

Firebase Realtime Database will be used to store the information required by the Learner Support Portal.

The planned database structure is:

```text
users/
tasks/
bookings/
scores/
resources/
```

A user's Firebase Authentication `uid` will be used where data needs to be associated with a specific learner.

---

## 4. Database Structure

### 4.1 Users

The `users` collection will contain basic information about registered learners.

```text
users/{uid}
    displayName
    email
    role
    createdAt
```

| Field         | Description                           |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `displayName` | Learner's display name                |
| `email`       | Learner's email address               |
| `role`        | User role within the application      |
| `createdAt`   | Date and time the account was created |

The `{uid}` value will correspond to the user's Firebase Authentication ID.

---

### 4.2 Tasks

The `tasks` collection will store learning and study tasks created by learners.

```text
tasks/{taskId}
    userId
    title
    category
    dueDate
    priority
    completed
    createdAt
```

| Field       | Description                         |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `userId`    | ID of the learner who owns the task |
| `title`     | Name of the task                    |
| `category`  | Task category                       |
| `dueDate`   | Task deadline                       |
| `priority`  | Task priority                       |
| `completed` | Whether the task has been completed |
| `createdAt` | Date and time the task was created  |

The `userId` field will be used to identify the learner who owns each task.

---

### 4.3 Support Bookings

The `bookings` collection will store requests for learner support sessions.

```text
bookings/{bookingId}
    userId
    topic
    preferredDate
    notes
    status
```

| Field           | Description                                 |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| `userId`        | Learner who made the booking                |
| `topic`         | Subject or area the learner needs help with |
| `preferredDate` | Requested support date                      |
| `notes`         | Additional information from the learner     |
| `status`        | Current booking status                      |

The `status` field can be used for values such as `pending`, `approved` or `completed`, depending on the final application requirements.

---

### 4.4 Game Scores

The `scores` collection will store results from the coding game.

```text
scores/{scoreId}
    userId
    score
    duration
    completedAt
```

| Field         | Description                          |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `userId`      | Learner who achieved the score       |
| `score`       | Game score                           |
| `duration`    | Time taken to complete the game      |
| `completedAt` | Date and time the game was completed |

This data can be used to display previous results and track learner performance.

---

### 4.5 Learning Resources

The `resources` collection will contain resources that learners can access from the portal.

```text
resources/{resourceId}
    title
    type
    url
    description
```

| Field         | Description                       |
| ------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `title`       | Resource name                     |
| `type`        | Type of resource                  |
| `url`         | Location of the resource          |
| `description` | Short description of the resource |

Resources may include websites, documents, videos or other learning material.

---

## 5. Firebase REST API

The Firebase Realtime Database REST API will be used to send requests between the website and Firebase.

The main HTTP methods are:

| Method   | Purpose                    |
| -------- | -------------------------- |
| `GET`    | Retrieve data              |
| `POST`   | Create a new record        |
| `PUT`    | Replace an existing record |
| `PATCH`  | Update selected fields     |
| `DELETE` | Remove a record            |

These methods will provide the database operations required by the application.

---

## 6. CRUD Operations

The task management feature will use CRUD operations.

### Create

A learner can create a new task.

```text
POST
```

The new task information will be sent to Firebase and stored under the `tasks` section.

### Read

The application can retrieve tasks belonging to the current learner.

```text
GET
```

The returned data will then be displayed on the learner's dashboard.

### Update

A learner can edit an existing task or change its completion status.

```text
PUT / PATCH
```

`PATCH` can be used when only specific fields need to be changed.

### Delete

A learner can remove a task after confirming the deletion.

```text
DELETE
```

---

## 7. Data Validation

Data will be checked before it is sent to Firebase.

Examples of validation include:

* Required fields must contain information.
* Text fields must contain valid values.
* Dates must use the expected format.
* Task information must be associated with a valid user.
* Invalid or incomplete requests should not be saved.

Validation will be handled on the application side, with Firebase Security Rules providing an additional level of protection.

---

## 8. Database Security

Firebase Security Rules will be used to control access to the Realtime Database.

The database should not allow unrestricted public access.

Where appropriate, learners should only be able to access and modify records belonging to their own account.

For example:

```text
Authenticated User
       ↓
Check User UID
       ↓
Compare with userId
       ↓
Allow or deny access
```

Different access levels may be required for learners, assessors or administrators. These permissions will depend on the final roles included in the project.

Private credentials and service-account files must not be committed to the project's GitHub repository.

---

## 9. Firebase Data Flow

The general data flow between the application and Firebase will be:

```text
┌──────────────┐
│    Learner   │
└──────┬───────┘
       │
       ↓
┌──────────────┐
│   Website    │
└──────┬───────┘
       │
       ↓
┌──────────────┐
│  JavaScript  │
└──────┬───────┘
       │
       ├──────────────→ Firebase Authentication
       │
       ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Realtime Database   │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ↓
      Website Display
```

For example, when a learner creates a task, the website collects the task information. JavaScript validates the input and sends the data to Firebase. The task is then stored in the database and can be retrieved later and displayed on the learner's dashboard.

---

## 10. Firebase Requirements

The Firebase implementation must provide the following:

* Firebase Authentication.
* Firebase Realtime Database.
* Firebase REST API communication.
* `GET` requests for retrieving information.
* `POST` requests for creating information.
* `PUT` or `PATCH` requests for updating information.
* `DELETE` requests for removing information.
* Authentication for protected operations.
* Firebase Security Rules.
* Input validation before data is stored.
* Appropriate separation of learner data.

---

## 11. Example Application Flow

A typical task creation process will work as follows:

```text
1. Learner logs in
        ↓
2. Firebase authenticates the learner
        ↓
3. Learner opens the task manager
        ↓
4. Learner enters task information
        ↓
5. JavaScript validates the information
        ↓
6. Application sends a POST request
        ↓
7. Firebase stores the task
        ↓
8. Application retrieves the updated data
        ↓
9. Task appears on the learner dashboard
```

This same approach can be adapted for other Firebase features such as support bookings and game scores.

---

## 12. Security Considerations

The following security practices will be followed:

* User authentication will be handled by Firebase Authentication.
* Passwords will not be stored in the Realtime Database.
* Database access will be controlled using Firebase Security Rules.
* Learners will not have unrestricted access to other learners' data.
* User input will be validated before being stored.
* Private credentials will not be placed in the source code.
* Service-account files will not be committed to GitHub.
* Database permissions will be tested before the application is submitted.

---

## 13. Conclusion

Firebase will provide the backend services required by the Learner Support Portal.

Firebase Authentication will manage learner accounts, while the Realtime Database will store application data such as tasks, support bookings, game scores and learning resources.

The REST API will allow the website to perform the required CRUD operations. Authentication, validation and Firebase Security Rules will be used to control access and protect the stored information.

This setup will provide the project with a suitable backend while keeping the database structure organised and easy to maintain.
